                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "238502937",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561198038146007",
                            "num_reviews": 260,
                            "playtime_forever": 2560,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 0,
                            "playtime_at_review": 2560,
                            "last_played": 1792889462
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I genuinely do not have enough good things to say about this title. I really don't. If you are looking for something that will allow you to escape from the everyday woes of life I don't know if I can think of a better title. This game has ZERO combat, it is a walking simulator through and through, but it reminds me deeply of that of the Elder Scrolls - in terms of its world, its characters and its music. The game is absolutely stunning and the goal is essentially to fulfill your mothers wishes and paint the 4 key pillars of beauty in the world of Eastshade. \n\nIt's fascinating when I think about it because I played the game twice. The first time it took me over 35 hours but if you absolutely speed rush it it can take as little as 3-4 but only if you know exactly what to do. It's very interesting in that way because you simply cannot do that if you don't know what to do or where to go. And that is something Eastshade does that most modern games would never even dream of doing. It doesn't give you a HUD, a UI, a map marker, a compass, or anything at all really. The game doesn't really tell you anything at all, aside from very vague journal entries and it's up to YOU to figure it out. I think that really is the point. I can't tell you how many times I did the wrong thing over and over before I figured out what to do. The game simply will not hold your hand. \n\nBut I think that is the profound beauty in a game like Eastshade. It WANTS you to get lost. It WANTS you to fail, and it wants you to try and try again until you finally figure it out. It's very slow, it's very calm, and  It's deeply rewarding. \n\nThere is something so profoundly exciting about discovering places that simply aren't marked on a map with path markers, (because there aren't any) it makes the entire thing feel like one absolutely massive adventure. \n\nEastshade has intriguing lore, mellifluous (if not melancholy) music, a gorgeous world, and it's simply the kind of world that will bring you peace, relax you, submerge you in sheer tranquility and ease you from the sorrows and tragedies of real life.",
